/-!
# Cartier complement of an affine-valued extension locus

Source: Milne, Abelian Varieties, Chapter I, proof of Lemma 3.3, printed pp.17–18,
finite-coordinate polar-divisor argument; Stacks Project, Divisors, Tags 02P0, 0AG8 and 0AGA
(blueprint: lem-sfb-factorial-affine-rational-poles).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The union of opens on which a dense-open map to a finite-type affine target extends
has an effective Cartier complement on a Noetherian integral scheme with factorial stalks.
The extension condition is equality on the intersection with the original domain. The
Cartier divisor may be empty; no properness or separatedness of the source is assumed.

Source: Milne, Abelian Varieties, Chapter I, proof of Lemma 3.3, printed pp.17–18,
expanded to finitely many affine coordinates; Stacks Project, Tags 02P0, 0AG8 and 0AGA
(blueprint: lem-sfb-factorial-affine-rational-poles).

Proof following the seven steps of the node:
1. Choose the generic point using lem-sc-scheme-sober. The lemma
   lem-sc-integral-scheme-stalks-in-function-field embeds all local rings and sections
   on nonempty opens into the function field K. Choose finitely many algebra generators
   of A, allowing an empty family. Apply lem-a1-locally-ringed-space-affine-adjunction
   to h and take generic germs to obtain their rational coordinate functions f_i.
2. A point is in the extension union exactly when every f_i belongs to its local ring.
   One implication takes germs of an extension. Conversely use def-weil-pbc-module-stalk
   to represent the finitely many germs on a common neighbourhood. Polynomial relations
   vanish because their generic germs vanish and sections inject into K by
   lem-sc-integral-scheme-stalks-in-function-field. Factor through A and apply the affine
   adjunction. The resulting map is over k, and agrees with h on the intersection since
   its coordinate generic germs agree. For no generators use the structural morphism.
3. Define the denominator ideal D_i on an open V by the regular sections c for which
   c f_i is regular. The injectivity into K gives gluing. For f_i = 0 this is the unit
   ideal. Otherwise, on a nonempty affine open, represent f_i = a/b with both a and b
   nonzero by lem-sc-integral-function-field-affine-fraction. They are nonzerodivisors
   in every stalk. Regard this as an invertible meromorphic section via
   def-sc-meromorphic-module, and apply lem-sc-hilbert-denominator-ideal-nowhere-dense
   to identify D_i as a quasi-coherent denominator ideal. Equality is tested in K.
4. Taking germs, and conversely representing c and c f_i near a point by
   def-weil-pbc-module-stalk, identifies the stalk ideal as {c : c f_i is regular}.
   It is the unit ideal exactly when f_i is regular, by testing c = 1.
5. In each factorial stalk cancel common prime factors to write f_i = u_i/b_i with
   coprime nonzero numerator and denominator; for f_i = 0 take b_i = 1. Prime
   factorization gives b_i dividing c u_i exactly when b_i divides c, also for c = 0.
   Hence the stalk denominator ideal is (b_i), with b_i nonzero. No affine coordinate
   ring is assumed factorial.
6. Form J as the finite product of the D_i using def-sbc-ideal-sheaf-powers, with
   empty product the unit ideal. By lem-weil-pbc-spec-tilde-stalk-localization and
   localization commuting with finite ideal products, J has stalk generated by the
   product of the b_i. This is a nonzerodivisor in the domain stalk, including the
   empty product 1. The lemma lem-weil-pbc-quasi-coherent-ideal-closed-subscheme
   constructs P. Apply lem-sfb-stalk-nonzerodivisor-cartier-spreading to obtain an
   effective Cartier divisor in the sense of def-pc-divisor-line-bundle.
7. The affine closed-subscheme description from
   lem-weil-pbc-quasi-coherent-ideal-closed-subscheme and the stalk localization from
   lem-weil-pbc-spec-tilde-stalk-localization show a point is outside P exactly when
   J has unit stalk. The product of the b_i is a unit exactly when every b_i is a
   unit, equivalently every f_i is regular. Step 2 identifies this with the extension
   union. Empty or everywhere regular coordinate families give the empty divisor.
   None of these steps uses properness of the target. -/
theorem exists_effectiveCartierCenter_compl_affine_extension_locus
    (k : Type u) [Field k]
    (W : Over (Spec (.of k))) [IsIntegral W.left]
    [IsLocallyNoetherian W.left] [CompactSpace W.left]
    (hfactorial : ∀ w : W.left, UniqueFactorizationMonoid (W.left.presheaf.stalk w))
    (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [Algebra k A] [Algebra.FiniteType k A]
    (H : W.left.Opens) (hH : Dense (H : Set W.left))
    (h : H.toScheme ⟶ Spec (.of A))
    (hh : h ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k A)) = H.ι ≫ W.hom) :
    ∃ (P : Scheme.{u}) (i : P ⟶ W.left), IsEffectiveCartierCenter i ∧
      Set.range i = ((sSup {V : W.left.Opens | ∃ hV : V.toScheme ⟶ Spec (.of A),
        hV ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k A)) = V.ι ≫ W.hom ∧
        W.left.homOfLE (inf_le_left : V ⊓ H ≤ V) ≫ hV =
          W.left.homOfLE (inf_le_right : V ⊓ H ≤ H) ≫ h} : W.left.Opens) :
        Set W.left)ᶜ := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
